WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.662 --> 00:00:09.470
- The state of 3D printing
today goes two ways,

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:11.430
there's people who are
developing machines that

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.570
can print with materials
that we currently

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:17.380
use through traditional
manufacturing processes,

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.930
and there's people that
are developing platforms

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.060
based on multiple materials.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:25.720
And this will enable a broad
range of new applications

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.730
by being able to
manufacture products

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.590
that we were not
able to manufacture

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:31.450
through traditional techniques.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.480
We've developed a low cost
multi material 3D printing

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.960
platform that can print
with up to 10 materials,

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.560
and it's machine vision enabled.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.100
So we use machine
vision techniques

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.220
to enhance, or amplify,
the capabilities

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.980
of current multi material
printing technologies.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.760
We call our project MultiFab.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.300
And with MultiFab you
integrate these two worlds

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.640
of traditional manufacturing,
and 3D printing.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.640
And by putting them
together you can

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.250
create a whole new
range of objects

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:02.100
that we haven't been
able to make to date.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.220
The machine vision system
integrated in MultiFab

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.950
allows us to easily
and very quickly

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.960
calibrate the printing system.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.650
Second, we use machine vision
to integrate existing components

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:18.880
into that printing process.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.090
So imagine we put
components into the printers

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.670
build platform, we
scan the objects,

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.240
and then we use that 3D
geometry information to print

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.960
the objects around
those existing objects.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.560
Right now a big portion
of the 3D printing kind

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.010
of hardware that's available
is focused on printing form,

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:40.660
and on objects for prototype.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.550
The holy grail is
to print things

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.740
that are fully functional
right out of the printer,

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:48.190
combining multiple materials
with many different properties,

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:50.290
but also existing
objects that have

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.000
some inherent functionality.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.550
We've printed lenses
on top of LED's.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.510
We put this existing
LED into a printer.

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:58.300
We print a Lens.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.900
On top.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.220
Designers and engineers are used
to designing with one material.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:08.610
So the whole design
paradigm, how we actually

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.100
designed products and
objects, will have to change.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.440
And that's going to require
new hardware, that's

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.930
going to require new
software, and that's going

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.420
to require whole new platforms.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.120
We're going to have to
change the way engineers

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.720
and designers think
about design and creating

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.830
products and objects.

